mudlle: MUD Language for Little Extensions
------------------------------------------

This software is Copyright (C) 1993-2012 David Gay and Gustav Hållberg.
Read the COPYRIGHT file for terms of use.


Installation
------------
To compile on Linux or macOS:

  make
  make compiler

macOS Setup
-----------
On macOS (Homebrew), install the required toolchain dependencies including LLVM:

  brew install llvm flex bison

Ensure LLVM tools (`llc`, `llvm-as`, `opt`) are available in your PATH:

  export PATH="$(brew --prefix llvm)/bin:$PATH"


Quick Introduction
------------------

mudlle is a small language designed for writing extensions to MUME, an
online multi-user game. It is heavily inspired by Scheme. This
distribution contains a stand-alone interpreter and compiler that can
be used to experiment with the language. It is up to you to integrate
it with your favourite application if you wish to use it as an
extension language.

Once compiled, run mudlle and check that basic stuff works:

  ./mudlle
  mudlle> 2+3
  Result: 5
  mudlle>

EOF (Ctrl-D), or evaluating quit() will exit the interpreter.

doc/mudlle-intro.doc and doc/mudlle-ref.doc contain a language introduction
and reference. doc/llvm-backend.md documents the LLVM backend.

LLVM Compiler Demo (Apple Silicon)
----------------------------------
Install LLVM and make its tools visible before building:

  brew install llvm
  export PATH="$(brew --prefix llvm)/bin:$PATH"
  make

On ARM64 and RISC-V, `load("xc.mud")` selects the LLVM backend. At the prompt,
compile a function to IR and then ask LLVM to produce host assembly:

  load("xc.mud")
  source = "fn (a, b) a + b";
  tree = mudlle_parse(source, null, false);
  ir = mc:compile_llvm(tree, 1, mc:source_code(source));
  w = writer:new_file("module.ll");
  writer:write(w, ir);
  writer:close(w);
  asm_tree = mudlle_parse(source, null, false);
  mc:compile_llvm_asm(asm_tree, "module.s");

Validate the IR with `llvm-as module.ll -o module.bc`. The LLVM backend leaves
the target triple and data layout open so LLVM uses the host architecture. On
x86-64, `load("xc.mud")` keeps the legacy compiler; load `llvmc.mud` to select
LLVM explicitly. Start a fresh Mudlle process with each loader when comparing
the backends.

Without LLVM installed, the interpreter remains available. Native x86-64
compilation continues to use the existing backend.

Validation and Compatibility
----------------------------
On an Apple Silicon Mac with Homebrew LLVM, `make` succeeds and
`./mudlle -e regression/llvm.mud` passes. The regression covers IR generation
for arithmetic, branches, and loops, then asks `llc` to emit ARM64 assembly.
A separate native harness calling the emitted addition function with tagged
inputs 3 and 5 returned the expected tagged result 7.

`mc:compile` follows the standard compile/prelink API, and the regression
exercises `mc:linkrun`, generated closures, optional and variadic arguments,
IR generation, and assembly output on ARM64. Full x64 parity remains in
progress; several primitive and floating-point operations still need coverage.

Use the complete regression suite's runtime to compare backend speed. Compare
the same tests and build configuration, and use timings only after confirming
that both backends pass the suite.

LLVM arithmetic dispatches float and bigint operations through Mudlle
primitives. Broader primitive/runtime coverage and matched x86-64 measurements
on amd64 remain outstanding.


